                <h2>Lead with the Conclusion: The Core Obstacle Is Inconsistent Accounting Standards</h2>
<p>The overseas advertising platform billing system differs fundamentally from domestic financial accounting logic. Top-up amount ≠ spend amount ≠ invoice amount — these three figures naturally have time gaps and statistical discrepancies.</p>
<p><strong>Data latency is the most direct cause.</strong> Platform daily spend is only confirmed the next day or even 48 hours later, while top-up records update in real time. Invoices follow the platform's own billing cycle (typically the calendar month of the ad serving timezone), which often misaligns with the company's financial reporting period. When a cross-border e-commerce team checks accounts mid-month, they often find sufficient account balance, yet the platform shows a 15%~20% difference between confirmed spend and invoiced amounts.</p>
<p><strong>Multi-account management amplifies reconciliation complexity.</strong> Mature overseas teams typically run 3~5 advertising accounts on a single platform, each tied to different campaigns or regions. Monthly consolidation requires verifying top-up records, spend reports, and invoice amounts account by account. Any missing data or inconsistent format in one link dramatically reduces overall reconciliation efficiency.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Three Key Steps for Unified Reconciliation</h2>
<h3>Step 1: Establish "Spend Confirmation" as the Accounting Baseline</h3>
<p>The anchor point for financial reconciliation should be the platform-confirmed spend amount, not the top-up amount. Top-ups are prepaid expenses; actual costs are based on spend. In practice, it's recommended to pull account spend reports every Friday (platforms typically support CSV export or API access) and perform two-way verification against financial system expense records.</p>
<p>An operations team for a utility app initially used the simple approach of "treating top-ups as expenses." They found the platform would refund unspent balances, but the finance team had already recorded the full amount as cost, causing quarterly reports to consistently show an 8%~12% variance from platform statements. After switching to spend-based confirmation, the variance dropped to within 2%.</p>
<h3>Step 2: Distinguish Between "Platform Statements" and "Financial Invoices" Time Windows</h3>
<p>Overseas advertising platforms typically issue previous month's formal statements and invoices on the 5th~10th business day of the following month. However, company tax filing and financial closing often require completion in early the following month, meaning finance must use estimated data for initial records and adjust later based on invoices.</p>
<p><strong>Practical recommendation:</strong> Establish a separate "accrued expenses" category in the financial system for advertising spend. After the 25th of each month, estimate the full-month amount based on platform cumulative spend and record as provisional entries. When invoices arrive the following month, make a one-time adjustment for the difference between provisional and actual invoice amounts. One independent site team reduced monthly reconciliation time from 3 person-days to 0.5 person-days using this method.</p>
<h3>Step 3: Build a Multi-Account Unified Dashboard</h3>
<p>If the team manages more than 3 advertising accounts, manual account-by-account reconciliation is extremely inefficient. Consider building a unified dashboard through:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Unified data format:</strong> Different platforms use different field names for spend (Meta calls it "Amount Spent," Google calls it "Cost"). Convert all to internal standard fields after export.</li>
<li><strong>Exchange rate handling:</strong> Top-ups and spend involve multiple currencies including USD, EUR, and GBP. Convert to RMB using the platform rate on the spend date to avoid significant fluctuations from month-end rate conversions.</li>
<li><strong>Automated reconciliation logic:</strong> When top-up amount minus spend amount &gt; zero, the system automatically marks it as prepaid balance. When spend amount &gt; top-up amount, it marks it as overdraft or credit usage.</li>
</ul>
<hr />
