<td>Recording at top-up date exchange rate</td>
<td>Month-end rate fluctuation causes variance</td>
<td>Use spend date rate consistently</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Ignoring platform rebates/discounts</td>
<td>Invoice amount &lt; top-up amount, reason unknown</td>
<td>Verify account Credit records</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Mixed multi-currency accounts</td>
<td>USD and CNY accounts used interchangeably</td>
<td>Top-up and spend must match the same currency account</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Refund cycle discrepancy</td>
<td>Refund receipt 2~4 weeks later than spend confirmation</td>
<td>Establish refund ledger tracking each refund's status</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<hr />
<h2>Final Thoughts</h2>
<p>The difficulty of overseas advertising reconciliation isn't the calculation itself, but the dispersion of data sources and diversity of accounting standards. Teams don't need to achieve 100% real-time matching of every figure, but rather establish a standardized process of "daily monitoring, weekly verification, monthly adjustment" that transforms financial reconciliation from reactive firefighting into an expected routine. If your team currently uses Excel to manually consolidate data across multiple accounts, prioritize evaluating reconciliation tools or systems to free up manpower for more core campaign optimization work.</p>
